Agent: That sounds like a meaningful project — researching a WWII-era refugee’s story can turn up a lot of context and sometimes surprising primary documents. Below I’ve put together a step‑by‑step plan, key resources (U.S., German, and international), practical tips on what to collect first, and how to request records. If you want, I can draft enquiry emails or a search checklist for you.

1) Gather everything you already know (the essentials)
- Full name (including maiden name / alternate spellings)  
- Date/place of birth (even approximate)  
- Approximate year of emigration / arrival in the U.S. (or range)  
- Ports (where she left from or arrived) or the U.S. city she first lived in  
- Any documents relatives may have (naturalization papers, old passports, ship tickets, letters, SSN, death certificate)  
- Names of relatives who came with her or sponsored/hosted her in the U.S.  
Why: archives and databases search best when you have precise identifiers; they also speed up record requests.

2) Key U.S. resources and searches
- USCIS Genealogy Program (Historical Records / Naturalization Files) — you can request naturalization files and passenger information; search their index and submit a genealogy request.  
- National Archives (NARA) — passenger arrival lists, visa files, displaced persons records, and immigration case files. NARA also holds many post‑war refugee/DP records. Look in the NARA catalog for “passenger lists 1945–1957,” “International Refugee Organization (IRO),” and DP camp records.  
- Ellis Island / Castle Garden if arrival might be earlier, or NARA passenger/crew lists for later arrivals (N.Y. arrivals 1897–1957). Many of these are searchable on Ancestry and FamilySearch.  
- Ancestry.com / FamilySearch.org / MyHeritage — searchable passenger lists, naturalization indexes, census records, Social Security Death Index (SSDI). FamilySearch is free and often has digitized records.  
- Local county courthouses / state archives in the U.S. where she lived — look for naturalization certificates, marriage licenses, death certificates and local newspapers.

3) Resources specific to displaced persons / post‑war refugees
- Arolsen Archives (formerly ITS, International Tracing Service) — the central archive for displaced persons records from WWII/afterwards; they have a searchable database and handle requests for personal files. Many DP camp and transit records are there.  
- International Refugee Organization (IRO) records — held in part by NARA and other archives; useful for refugees processed 1946–1952.  
- American Jewish Joint Distribution Committee (JDC) archives — if your great‑aunt was Jewish or assisted by JDC, their records can be invaluable.  
- Red Cross tracing / ICRC archives — tracing and relief records

4) German / local European sources
- Standesamt (civil registry) in the municipality of birth — birth and marriage records (you may need the town name and will often have to request via mail or the local archive).  
- Bundesarchiv (Federal Archives) — broader documents, but more for military/official records.  
- Regional archives (Landesarchive) and Kirchenbuch (church/parish) records if civil records are missing.  
- Local city archives or historical societies in her German hometown for newspapers, refugee/evacuation records.  
Note: German civil records may require proof of relation or a legitimate research purpose; each office has its own process.

5) Holocaust / persecution‑era resources (if relevant)
- US Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) databases and collections — survivor databases, transport lists, and testimonies  
- Yad Vashem (Israel) — Pages of Testimony, transport and other records  
These are relevant if the family’s experience involved persecution or Holocaust displacement.

6) Practical steps / how to request records
A. Start at home: collect scans/photos of any documents and write a short family timeline.  
B. Search online databases: Ancestry, FamilySearch, Arolsen, NARA catalog, USCIS Genealogy index, USHMM databases. Try different name spellings and date ranges.  
C. Submit formal requests:  
   - USCIS Genealogy Program: submit their genealogy request form for naturalization files.  
   - Arolsen Archives: search their database and request records or copies; they have a researcher service.  
   - NARA: identify the relevant record group and request copies or plan a visit.  
D. Contact local archives in Germany (Standesamt) where she was born — if you don’t know the town, try to narrow it via passenger lists or family stories.  
E. Reach out to relatives and community groups (churches, ethnic societies) — oral histories and family letters often supply critical clues.

7) Tips & practical notes
- Name variations: German names often appear with anglicized spellings after immigration; also check for middle names and maiden names.  
- Passenger lists often show last residence, port of departure, ship name and arrival port — very helpful.  
- If she arrived in the late 1940s as a DP/refugee, look specifically in DP/IRE/IRO and Arolsen records rather than pre‑war passenger lists.  
- Privacy and access: some archives require proof of relationship to release certain personal files; others provide redacted indexes freely.  
- Time & cost: some searches and certified copies cost money and can take weeks; plan accordingly.

8) Oral‑history and community resources
- Conduct family interviews and record them (audio/video). Ask older relatives for names, places, dates and stories.  
- Local historical/cultural societies (German‑American clubs, churches) often have memoirs, newsletters and community records.  
- Consider DNA genealogy (if you’re open to it) to connect with living relatives in the regions she left.

9) Want help drafting requests or searches?
If you’d like, I can:  
- Draft an email/request template for USCIS, NARA, or a German Standesamt.  
- Suggest exact search queries for Ancestry/FamilySearch (name + approximate dates + “Germany” + “displaced person/D.P.”).  
- Help organize the family timeline from what you already know.

If you share whatever details you have (full name, maiden name, approximate birth year, year/place of arrival if known, U.S. first city of residence), I can outline a prioritized search plan with the most likely record sources and sample search phrases to use. Would you like me to do that?
